### Java Method for MD5 Hashing

```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;

public class Md5Hasher {
    public static String getMd5Hash(String input) {
        try {
            MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            byte[] digest = md.digest(input.getBytes());

            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            for (byte b : digest) {
                sb.append(String.format("%02x", b));
            }
            return sb.toString();

        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            System.err.println("Error: MD5 algorithm not found");
            return null;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String hash = getMd5Hash("Hello, World!");
        if (hash != null) {
            System.out.println("MD5 Hash: " + hash);
        }
    }
}
```

### Trade-offs and Recommendations

- **Performance**: ChaCha20 is generally faster on systems without hardware AES acceleration.
- **Security**: AES-256 offers stronger security than AES-128 but may incur a performance penalty. GCM mode provides authenticated encryption, which is crucial for data integrity.
- **Concurrency**: Ensure all cryptographic operations are thread-safe to avoid race conditions in high-concurrency environments.

